<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Husayn Bayqara</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/1469">1469</a>–<a href="/years/1506">1506</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Husain Bayqara</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Husayn Bayqara was a Timurid ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a> who reigned from 1469 to 1506. His court was a brilliant centre of Persian and Chagatai literature, painting and architecture, with figures such as the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-shir-navai">Ali-Shir Nava'i</a> and the painter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamal-ud-din-behzad">Bihzad</a>. His reign marked the cultural high point of the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timurid-empire">Timurid Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/husayn-bayqara.jpg" alt="Husayn Bayqara" width="262" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kamāl ud-Dīn Behzād · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Sultan_Husayn_Mirza_Bayqara_at_the_age_of_about_50_years._1500%E2%80%9325_copy_from_Behz%C4%81d%27s_original_of_about_1490.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Husayn Bayqara was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a> in June or July 1438. His father, Ghiyas ud-din Mansur Mirza, was a great-grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> and died when Husayn was seven or eight, and the boy took the name Bayqara from his grandfather Bayqara Mirza I. After the death of the Timurid ruler of Herat, Abul-Qasim Babur Mirza, in 1457, Khurasan fell into anarchy, divided among many rivals. Husayn lived for some ten years as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenary</a> around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merv">Merv</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khwarazm">Khwarazm</a>, raiding Khurasan and often in dire straits. He took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorgan">Jurjan</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kara-koyunlu">Kara Koyunlu</a> in 1458 but could not hold it against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-said-mirza">Abu Sa'id Mirza</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-said-mirza">Abu Sa'id Mirza</a> was defeated and executed after the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karabakh">Qarabagh</a>, Husayn besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a> and captured it on 24 March 1469. He defeated Yadgar Muhammad Mirza, a protege of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aq-qoyunlu">Aq Qoyunlu</a> leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzun-hasan">Uzun Hasan</a>, at Chenaran on 15 September 1469. Yadgar occupied Herat on 7 July 1470, but Husayn retook it six weeks later, and his realm was then secure, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxus">Oxus</a> as the border with the other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timurid-empire">Timurids</a>. He faced revolts, among them one by his eldest son Badi' al-Zaman, who besieged Herat in 1499. He suffered from palsy for twenty years of his reign and banned the drinking of wine and the shaving of beards.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Husayn's court was a centre of literature, with the poets <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jami">Jami</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-shir-navai">Ali-Shir Nava'i</a>, the last great classical Persian poet and the founder of Chagatai Turkic literature respectively. About half of all known Timurid building works were carried out in his reign, mostly around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a>, where he built a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasa</a> and a mausoleum in the Musalla complex, in which he was buried, and the palace Bāḡ-e Jahān-ārā. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbeks</a> conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transoxiana">Transoxiana</a> in 1501. Husayn began to march against them but died in 1506, just after setting out. His sons Badi' al-Zaman and Muzaffar Husain disputed the succession. The next year <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-shaybani">Muhammad Shaybani</a> took Herat, ending Timurid rule in Khurasan. His cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babur">Babur</a> admired his court.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Husayn Bayqara” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Herat under <b>Husayn Bayqara</b> (r. 1469–1506) was arguably the most brilliant literary and artistic court of the fifteenth-century Islamic world.</blockquote><a href="/empire/timurid-empire">Timurid Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>. Died in Baba Ilahi. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.34,62.2&amp;year=1487" aria-label="Herat on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Husayn Bayqara"><image href="/maps/globes/g060n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5156 4628h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5156 4628h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=34.34,62.2&amp;year=1487">What was here in 1487? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
